course: dictionary
lesson: 5139
title: Involve and be involved
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To involve something means to include it as a necessary part. «A surprise party involves small lies» means the small lies are a part of the party that you cannot leave out. Look at the words around it to find the clue: a party with lies in it.
explain: To involve a person means to make them take part. «Involve everyone in the game» means make sure every single person joins in. The same word works with things and with people, so check which one comes after it.
explain: «Be involved in» means to take part in something, or to be connected with it, often in trouble. «A judge must not judge a case in which his family is involved» means the judge's family is connected to the case, so the judge cannot be fair. The clue is the word case, which is a problem that needs fixing.
fact: «Involve» is a verb, so its forms are involve, involves, involved and involving.
fact: «Involves» means includes as a necessary part, as in «The job involves lifting boxes».
fact: «Involve everyone» means make everyone take part.
fact: «Be involved in» means take part in, or be connected with something, often trouble.
fact: A quick check is to swap in «include» or «take part in» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «Surprise parties involve small lies for a happy reason.» In this sentence, what does involve mean? | Throw away | Forget completely | Make very quiet | *Include as a necessary part
exercise: Mrs Ahmed says, «Let's involve everyone in the game.» What does she want? | Make the game shorter | Give a prize to only one team | Make everyone leave the room | *Make everyone take part
exercise: «Two cars were involved in the crash.» What does involved in suggest here? | Were parked near a shop | Were sold to a garage | *Were connected with the crash | Were painted bright red
test: Ben wrote, «The game involved three teams last year.» Which form of involve is used here? | involving | *involved | involves | involve
test: «She was involved in the school play.» What does this mean? | *She took part in the school play | She fell asleep during the play | She had nothing to do with the play | She wrote the name of the play
exam: In maths, «a problem involving fractions» means which? | A fraction that is too big to solve | A problem with no numbers | *A problem that uses fractions | A problem a teacher has not set
